"""Grade 9 life essentials: who I am becoming online and at school."""
from spec import L, U

N9 = U('Who am I becoming?', 'Morality', 'How do I stay myself with a phone in my hand and a group watching?', [
L('Screens, comparison and who you are becoming',
  'Your feed is built to make you compare, and comparison is the engine of envy. The Church has a very old language for this: dignity that comes from God and not from likes, the tenth commandment against envy and endless wanting, and purity of heart as a way of seeing. The lesson is practical: what you give your attention to trains what you love.',
  '1700-1703,2534-2540,2544-2547,2518-2519,2521-2523,2729',
  'Psalm 139:13-16;Galatians 1:10;Matthew 6:1-6;Philippians 4:8',
  'dignity image-of-god virtue humility',
  'When you scroll, who do you end up comparing yourself to, and how do you feel afterward?',
  'Do a three-day “attention audit.” Each time you pick up your phone, tally the reason (bored, anxious, lonely, habit, a real reason). On day three, circle your top two reasons and choose one concrete change, such as no phone in the first twenty minutes of the morning, or one app moved off the home screen.',
  'morning-offering',
  id='9-L1',
  hook='Hold up two photos of the same person: one filtered and posed, one candid and tired. Ask: “Which one do people trust? Which one do people like? Which one is the real you?” Then read Psalm 139:14: “I praise you, for I am wondrously made.”',
  teach=[
    'Start with where your worth comes from. The Catechism says every human being is made “in the image of God” and has a dignity that cannot be earned or lost (CCC 1700–1703). That means your worth is not decided by a number on a screen. Paul puts it bluntly: “If I were still pleasing men, I should not be a servant of Christ” (Galatians 1:10).',
    'Now see how envy works. The tenth commandment is about desire that has lost its way: wanting what others have, and being sad at their good (CCC 2534–2540). Social media is an envy machine because it shows highlights beside your behind-the-scenes. The Catechism says envy is a capital sin and calls the baptized to fight it by good will and by “goodwill toward others” (CCC 2538–2540).',
    'The Church’s cure is not to want nothing, but to want God first: “poverty of heart” (CCC 2544–2547). When your deepest desire is to be loved by God, comparison loses some of its sting. The sixth Beatitude, “Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God,” is about seeing rightly, what you look at and how (CCC 2518–2519), and modesty guards the privacy of the person (CCC 2521–2523).',
    'Attention is the practical edge. Distraction in prayer, says the Catechism, shows “what we are attached to” (CCC 2729). If a notification can take you out of anything, notice that. Freedom here is small and daily: choose when you look, and why. No one is asking you to throw the phone away; they are asking you to stay in charge of it.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What is one account or app that makes you feel worse about yourself? Why do you keep it?',
    'What is the difference between wanting something good and envying someone?',
    'Who in your life is the same online and offline? What do you notice about them?',
    'What would you do with an extra hour a day if you chose when to use your phone?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'Where does your worth come from, according to the Church?|The number of friends you have|Being made in the image of God|Your grades|Your looks|1|CCC 1700–1703.',
    'What is envy?|Wanting something good|Sadness at another’s good|Admiring someone|Competing fairly|1|CCC 2539.',
    'What does the Church offer as the cure for endless wanting?|Poverty of heart and a desire for God|Buying less|Being stricter|Avoiding all people who have more|0|CCC 2544–2547.',
  ],
  life='For one week, choose one attention rule: for example, no phone for the first twenty minutes of the day, phones out of the room at meals, or one app turned off at night. Keep a tally on a card. On the last day write three lines on what you noticed.',
  saint='Carlo Acutis: a teenager from Milan who loved computers, built a website about Eucharistic miracles, and died of leukemia at 15 in 2006. He was canonized in 2025.',
  misc=[
    '“Envy is just wanting what other people have, which is normal.”',
    '“Online is not real life, so it does not count.”',
    '“Using my phone is neutral.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:chastity', 'room:desales-devout-life'],
  mem='“I praise you, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made.” (Psalm 139:14)',
  assess='Ask each student to explain in their own words where worth comes from and how envy works, and to name the attention rule they chose.',
  script=dict(
    open='Show two photos of the same person, one filtered and posed, one candid. Ask, “Which one would get more likes? Which one is more real?” Let them argue. Then say, “Today is about who gets to define you.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “Start here. The Church says you are made in the image of God. That is not a nice phrase; it means your worth is built in and cannot be earned or lost. Likes cannot add to it and a bad day cannot remove it. Paul says, ‘If I were still trying to please people, I would not be a servant of Christ.’ He is saying: someone else is the audience.”',
      'Say: “Envy is a sneaky sin. The tenth commandment is about wanting what others have and being sad that they have it. Social media makes this easy: you see their highlights next to your behind the scenes. The Catechism says to fight envy with goodwill, and with humility. That means choosing to be glad for people.”',
      'Say: “The Church’s answer is not ‘want nothing.’ It is ‘want God first.’ The Catechism calls it poverty of heart. If the deepest thing you want is to be loved by God, you are less hungry for approval. And remember the Beatitude, ‘Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.’ What we look at shapes what we want.”',
      'Say: “Attention is where this gets practical. Distraction in prayer shows what we are attached to. Notice what you reach for. You do not need to throw your phone away. You need to be in charge of it. So we will do a small experiment: one rule for one week.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'Whose life makes you feel behind? What are you not seeing?',
      'When is the last time you were truly glad for someone else’s success? What did it feel like?',
      'What rule can you try for one week?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Give each student a card. Write at the top, “My attention rule.” Write the rule in one sentence.',
      'Write one thing you will do with the time you gain.',
      'Pair up and promise to ask each other on day four how it is going.',
    ],
    close='Pray the morning offering together. Say, “You are not a number. Spend your attention on what you love.”'),
  tryit=['Tally why you pick up your phone.', 'Choose one attention rule for a week.', 'Name what you will do with the time.'],
  saintStory='Carlo Acutis was born in London in 1991 and raised in Milan. He was a normal teenager in many ways: he loved soccer, video games, dogs and computers, and he made friends easily. He was also unusually serious about Mass. His mother, who had not gone to church much, said he led her back to the faith.\nCarlo taught himself to code, and used his skills to build a website cataloguing Eucharistic miracles around the world, which he worked on for about three years. He said, “Everyone is born an original, but many people end up dying as photocopies.” He gave his pocket money to help the homeless and was kind to classmates who were bullied or left out.\nIn October 2006, he was diagnosed with a very aggressive leukemia and died within days, aged 15. He offered his suffering for the Pope and the Church. He was beatified in 2020 and canonized on September 7, 2025.',
),

L('Group chats, gossip and the courage to tell the truth',
  'Most cruelty today travels by message: a screenshot, a joke at someone’s expense, a rumor. The eighth commandment and the Church’s teaching on scandal take this seriously. Silence in a group chat is also a choice. This lesson gives students words, rules and courage to refuse to pile on and to stand with the person being talked about.',
  '2464-2470,2475-2487,2284-2287,1808,1816,2477-2479',
  'Ephesians 4:25-32;James 3:1-12;Matthew 18:15-17;Luke 6:31',
  'justice virtue fortitude',
  'What is the worst thing you have seen in a group chat, and what did you do?',
  'Write three short, ready-to-use sentences you can send when a chat turns on someone (for example, “Not cool. I am out of this topic.” or “Let’s talk to them, not about them.”). Then write one sentence you could say in person. Role-play with a partner.',
  'serenity',
  id='9-L2',
  hook='Pass a sheet of paper around the room. Each student writes one sentence about an imaginary person “Alex.” Read the sheet aloud as one story. Ask: “What do we think of Alex now? Who has actually met Alex?”',
  teach=[
    'The eighth commandment protects the truth and a person’s good name (CCC 2464–2470). The Catechism names the sins that damage reputation: rash judgment (assuming the worst), detraction (revealing a person’s faults to those who have no need to know), and calumny (lying to harm) (CCC 2477). A true story told just to hurt is still a sin. The rule of charity: interpret the neighbor’s words and actions favorably, or ask them (CCC 2478).',
    'Then there is scandal. “Scandal is an attitude or behavior which leads another to do evil” (CCC 2284). It is graver when it comes from those who by role or influence should lead well, and the Catechism says that those who lead others into sin by pressure, mockery or example carry some responsibility (CCC 2285–2287). In a group chat, a joke that gets other people to join in cruelty is scandal.',
    'Courage is the virtue that carries this. Fortitude “ensures firmness in difficulties and constancy in the pursuit of the good” (CCC 1808). It includes the courage to say, in a small way, “That’s not okay” (CCC 1816). The Gospel gives a practical way to confront: go privately first, then bring someone with you (Matthew 18:15–17). And the Golden Rule is the quick test: would I want this said about me? (Luke 6:31).',
    'Bystanders matter. Silence can feel safe, but the Catechism says we share in others’ sins by “taking part in them directly,” “ordering or advising,” “approving,” “not disclosing,” or “not hindering” (CCC 1868). You cannot fix everything, but you can take one step: leave, speak, message the person, or tell a trusted adult. That is not tattling; it is protecting someone.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'Is it gossip if it is true? When is it okay to share something unflattering about someone?',
    'What makes it so hard to say, “Stop”?',
    'What is the difference between telling an adult to get someone help and “tattling”?',
    'Who was kind to you when others were not? What did they do?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What is detraction?|Telling lies to hurt someone|Revealing someone’s faults without good reason|Praising someone|Keeping a secret|1|CCC 2477.',
    'What does the Catechism say about leading others into sin by example or pressure?|It is harmless|It is called scandal and carries responsibility|It only matters for adults|It is not a sin|1|CCC 2284–2287.',
    'What is one step the Gospel gives for correcting someone?|Post about it|Go to them privately first|Wait a year|Ignore it|1|Matthew 18:15.',
  ],
  life='This week, pick one chat or group you are in. If it turns on someone, say one of your ready-made sentences. Also send one private message to someone who has been left out: “Hey, want to sit with us?” or “I thought you did a good job.”',
  saint='Joan of Arc: a teenage girl who told the truth to the judges who tried her, even when it cost her life.',
  misc=[
    '“If I did not start it, it is not my business.”',
    '“Joking does not count.”',
    '“Telling an adult means I am a snitch.”',
  ],
  deeper=['room:aug-moral'],
  mem='“Let no evil talk come out of your mouths, but only such as is good for edifying.” (Ephesians 4:29)',
  assess='Ask each student to name the three sins against reputation, give one example, and show the sentence they would use to refuse.',
  script=dict(
    open='Pass a sheet of paper around the circle, each student adding one sentence about an imaginary person called Alex. Read it aloud. Ask, “How well do we know Alex? What have we decided about them?”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “The eighth commandment is ‘You shall not bear false witness.’ It started with courts, but it reaches into every group chat. The Catechism says every person has a right to the truth and to a good name. When you shape what other people think of someone, you are holding something that belongs to them.”',
      'Say: “Three sins to know. Rash judgment: you assume the worst without evidence. Detraction: you tell someone’s faults, even true ones, to people who have no reason to know. Calumny: you lie to hurt. Notice detraction can be true. A true story told to hurt is still harm. And there is a test in the Catechism: interpret people favourably, or ask them.”',
      'Say: “Scandal is another word to know. It means leading someone else to do wrong by what we do or say. If your joke makes five people join in, you have led them. The Catechism says whoever uses their influence to lead others into evil is responsible. Silence is a choice too. You are never forced to join.”',
      'Say: “Courage is the answer. It does not have to be a speech. It can be one sentence: ‘Not cool.’ Or a private message to the person. Or leaving the chat. Jesus’ rule for conflict is to go to the person first. And if someone is being hurt badly, telling a trusted adult is not snitching; it is protecting.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'Why is it so easy to join in? What makes it hard to stop?',
      'What is a sentence you could actually say?',
      'Who could you invite in this week?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Hand out cards. Write three ready-to-use sentences for refusing to pile on.',
      'In pairs, role-play: one person says something cruel about a classmate, the other uses a sentence.',
      'Swap, then write one kind message you will send today.',
    ],
    close='Pray the serenity prayer together. Say, “Courage is a muscle. Use it small, and use it today.”'),
  tryit=['Write three sentences you can send to stop a pile-on.', 'Role-play one with a partner.', 'Write the kind message you will send today.'],
  saintStory='Joan was born around 1412 in Domrémy, a village in eastern France, during the Hundred Years’ War. She could not read or write. When she was about thirteen she began to hear voices she believed were St. Michael, St. Catherine and St. Margaret, telling her to help the dauphin, Charles, claim his throne and drive out the English.\nAt sixteen she persuaded a skeptical captain to send her to the king. After examination by theologians at Poitiers, she was given command of troops and lifted the siege of Orléans in 1429, only nine days after arriving. Charles was crowned at Reims. In 1430 she was captured by Burgundians and sold to the English, who put her on trial for heresy at Rouen.\nFor months she was questioned by learned men who tried to trap her. She answered plainly and refused to deny what she believed she had been told. She was condemned and burned on May 30, 1431, aged about nineteen. Twenty-five years later a new trial found her innocent. She was canonized in 1920.',
),

L('Stress, anxiety and learning to trust',
  'Many teenagers carry more stress than anyone admits: grades, friends, family, the future. The faith does not pretend this is easy. It offers three things at once: a theology of trust in a Father who provides, a practical rhythm of rest and prayer, and a clear permission to ask for help, including professional help. Anxiety is not a lack of faith.',
  '302-314,305,2828-2830,2835-2837,2288-2289,164,2184',
  'Matthew 6:25-34;Psalm 131;Philippians 4:6-7;1 Kings 19:1-8',
  'prayer hope peace creation',
  'What is the biggest thing weighing on you this month, and where do you feel it in your body?',
  'Make a “worry sort” with three columns: what I can do something about, what I can ask someone to help with, and what I have to hand over. For each item write one next step or one sentence of prayer. Choose one thing from the first column to do tonight.',
  'psalm-23',
  id='9-L3',
  hook='Ask students to rate their stress from 1 to 10 on a card and not show it. Then read 1 Kings 19:1–8: Elijah runs away, collapses under a tree and asks to die. God sends an angel with bread and water and lets him sleep first. Ask: “What did God do first?”',
  teach=[
    'Be clear first: anxiety is common and not a sin, and it is not a failure of faith. The Catechism teaches that we have a duty to care for our health: “Life and physical health are precious gifts entrusted to us by God. We must take reasonable care of them” (CCC 2288). That includes sleep, food, exercise, friends and, when needed, doctors and counselors. Prayer does not replace care; it goes with it.',
    'The heart of the Christian answer is providence: God “guides his creation to its perfection” and cares for each person (CCC 302–305). Jesus asks us to live with the trust of a child: “Do not be anxious about your life” (Matthew 6:25; CCC 305). That is not “do not feel.” It is an invitation to hand over what we cannot control. The petition “Give us this day our daily bread” is about taking one day at a time (CCC 2828–2830, 2835–2837).',
    'Rest matters. Elijah is the model: God feeds him and lets him sleep before giving any instruction (1 Kings 19). The Sabbath command is a gift: the world is not held up by you (CCC 2184). A short, regular practice is more helpful than a long one you skip: five minutes of prayer in the evening, a walk, one phone-free hour.',
    'Faith itself can be tested by hard times. The Catechism says our experiences of evil and suffering “can shake our faith and become a temptation against it,” and invites us to keep clinging to God even when feeling is absent (CCC 164). If anxiety or low mood is heavy or lasts, talk to a parent, a school counselor, a doctor or a priest. If you ever think of harming yourself, tell an adult right away, or contact a crisis line. That is exactly what a good Christian does.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What is the difference between caring and worrying?',
    'What helps you calm down: sleep, music, exercise, a friend? How could you build more of it?',
    'What does it mean to hand something over to God in practice?',
    'Who could you tell if the stress felt too heavy?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What does the Catechism say about our health?|It does not matter|We must take reasonable care of it as a gift from God|Only the soul matters|Illness is always a punishment|1|CCC 2288.',
    'What did God do first for Elijah in 1 Kings 19?|Scolded him|Let him sleep and gave him food|Sent him to a battle|Gave a lecture|1|1 Kings 19:5–8.',
    'Is anxiety a sign of weak faith?|Yes|No, it is common and can be treated with care, prayer and help|Only for the unholy|Only in teens|1|CCC 2288 and 164: faith can be tested; seek help.',
  ],
  life='Choose one small daily rhythm: five minutes of quiet at night, a walk, or one phone-free hour. Also name one trusted adult you would tell if the stress got too heavy, and write their name on a card in your wallet.',
  saint='Thérèse of Lisieux: a sensitive girl who cried easily, struggled with scruples, and found peace in a “little way” of trust.',
  misc=[
    '“If I had enough faith, I would not be anxious.”',
    '“Asking for help means I am weak.”',
    '“God does not care about small worries.”',
  ],
  deeper=['room:therese-story-of-a-soul', 'apol:suffering'],
  mem='“Do not be anxious about anything, but in everything by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving let your requests be made known to God.” (Philippians 4:6)',
  assess='Ask each student to explain the difference between care and worry, and to name one practice and one person they will turn to.',
  script=dict(
    open='Ask students to write their stress level from 1 to 10 on a card and fold it. Say, “No one will see it. Just notice it.” Then read 1 Kings 19:1–8 aloud. Ask, “What did God do first? A speech? A lesson?” (He fed him and let him sleep.)',
    teach=[
      'Say: “Anxiety is common. You may feel it more than you let on. It is not a sin, and it does not mean your faith is weak. Elijah was a great prophet and he ran away, lay down and said, ‘Let me die.’ God’s first move was food and sleep. So begin with your body: sleep, food, movement.”',
      'Say: “The Church takes health seriously. The Catechism, 2288, says life and health are precious gifts and we should take reasonable care of them. That includes seeing a doctor or counselor. Prayer and help are not rivals. Both are ways God cares for you.”',
      'Say: “Then trust. Jesus says, ‘Do not be anxious about your life.’ He is not telling you not to feel. He is telling you who is holding the world. The Our Father says ‘Give us this day our daily bread.’ Notice it is for today. We are asked to take one day at a time. The Catechism calls this the trust of a child.”',
      'Say: “Sometimes faith feels dry or heavy. The Catechism, 164, admits our experiences of suffering can shake our faith. The answer is not to pretend. It is to keep showing up, and to tell a trusted adult. If you ever think about hurting yourself, tell someone right away. That is courage, not weakness.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What are the top three things pressing on you right now?',
      'What actually helps you calm down? What do you do that makes it worse?',
      'Who is the one adult you would tell, and what would you say?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Give each student a sheet with three columns: Can do, Can ask for help, Hand over.',
      'Fill it out for the biggest worries. Circle one thing from the first column to do tonight.',
      'Write a one-sentence prayer for the third column. Keep it on your phone or desk.',
    ],
    close='Pray the Twenty-third Psalm slowly. Say, “You are held. Take one step and ask for help.”'),
  tryit=['Sort worries into do, ask, hand over.', 'Pick one thing to do tonight.', 'Name the adult you would tell.'],
  saintStory='Thérèse Martin was born in Alençon, France, in 1873, the youngest of nine children. Her mother died when she was four, and she became a very sensitive child who cried easily and was often afraid. In her teens she struggled with scruples, an anxious sense that she was always doing wrong.\nOn Christmas night in 1886, aged thirteen, she felt that something changed: she was able to stop crying over small things and to think of others. She asked the Pope in person for permission to enter the Carmelite convent at fifteen, and got it. She spent nine hidden years at Lisieux.\nShe taught what she called the little way: doing small things with great love and trusting that God, like a father, lifts the little ones. In the last year of her life she suffered from tuberculosis and a deep darkness in her faith. She died on September 30, 1897, aged 24. Her autobiography, Story of a Soul, made her one of the most loved saints of the twentieth century.',
),
])
